  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The department's main river, the Argens winds between Le Muy and Roquebrune-sur-Argens before reaching the sea at Fréjus, at the foot of the Rocher de Roquebrune. The Roquebrune tourist office presents it as one of the Var's last largely wild rivers, to explore by canoe, kayak or paddleboard.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The Roquebrune-sur-Argens tourist office says swimming is allowed in the Argens, but there is no developed or supervised site: a campsite in the town specifies that no bathing site is declared there. No dog rule is published for these banks; a Pays de Fayence guide points out that, unless a municipal order is posted, riverbanks allow dogs to swim. Water level and flow vary a lot with the season, and water quality can be degraded after heavy rain: look at the signs and the town's orders.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: pick a quiet bank, keep your dog on a leash near the water and don't let it swim after a storm.</div>

      <h2>The Louis Go water-sports base</h2>
      <p>The Louis Go municipal water-sports base is at the entrance of the "Bagarrèdes" defile, among the arkosic sandstone cliffs of the Rocher de Roquebrune (RD 25, Rabinon district, 83490 Le Muy). The Var Tourisme listing, updated on 17/06/2026 by the Dracénie Provence Verdon tourist office, shows "Animals accepted" among the services, along with a picnic area, parking, a water point and accessible toilets. It does not say whether this extends to the boats.</p>
      <p>The base rents canoes, kayaks and paddleboards by the hour, half-day or day (kayak €9 to €38, canoe €13 to €50, paddleboard €11 to €33), with a state-certified instructor on request. You must be able to swim and be at least one metre tall. Open from 1 May to 30 September 2026: in June and September Tuesday to Sunday 11am-5pm, in July and August Tuesday to Saturday 9am-6pm and Sunday 10am-5pm, restricted hours on public holidays. Contact: 06 77 67 09 51. The Le Muy town hall bans fires and smoking around the water-sports areas, a protected natural zone.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Rocher de Roquebrune, a red sandstone massif, overlooks the valley and offers lovely views to extend the outing on foot. At Le Muy, the WAM PARK leisure base at Lac du Rabinon states on its website that dogs are allowed but must be on a leash, that entry is free and that swimming is not allowed there.</p>
    </div>

